# AGENTS.md

## New Minecraft Mod Project Protocol

- Before starting any new Minecraft mod project, read `~/.config/opencode/PROJECT_PLANNING.md`
- Ask all clarifying questions from the checklist before writing code
- Propose complete architecture and wait for confirmation before implementing
- If making 3+ commits that change the same core mechanism (trigger, area, timing), pause and ask for detailed requirements

## Browser Launching

- When the user asks to open a browser, immediately launch a new, separate Chrome instance. Never kill, quit, restart, or attach to any Chrome window or instance not created in the current session — other opencode sessions own their browsers (including whatever holds port 9222), and the user's main Chrome is untouchable. Do not inspect or otherwise manage Chrome instances launched by other sessions.
- Do not report on or worry about other sessions' browsers unless the user asks about them.
- Never launch or attach the `chrome-devtools-profile` server — it is configured to the user's main Chrome profile.
- Never default the launch recipe to port 9222. Before launching, verify the chosen port is actually free — e.g. `curl -s --max-time 2 http://127.0.0.1:$PORT/json/version` fails with connection refused, or `lsof -iTCP:$PORT -sTCP:LISTEN` prints nothing. If 9222 is held by anything else, launch on a different free port — never launch with 9222 args while another instance holds it (the debugger silently fails to bind, and the `chrome-devtools-existing` tools, pinned to 9222, may then attach to whoever holds the port). An instance launched earlier in this same session on 9222 may be reused via the `chrome-devtools-existing` tools instead of launching another.
- Working recipe (macOS), with `$PORT` verified free first:
  `open -na "Google Chrome" --args --remote-debugging-port=$PORT --remote-allow-origins='*' --user-data-dir="$(mktemp -d)/chrome-profile-$PORT" --incognito --no-first-run --no-default-browser-check "<URL>"`
  The fresh `--user-data-dir` is required because modern Chrome refuses remote debugging on the default profile. `--incognito` means no persisted logins — the user authenticates by hand.
- After any launch meant to be driven via the `chrome-devtools-existing` tools, first verify the attached browser is the one just launched — list pages and confirm the expected URL/tab is present. If the tabs are not yours, disconnect immediately — never drive another session's browser.
- When the `chrome-devtools` MCP toolsets are unavailable (a foreign browser on the pinned port, profile locks, leftover browsers), drive Chrome directly over the DevTools protocol with the node driver bundled in the global `outlook-webmail-teams` skill:
  `node ~/.config/opencode/skills/outlook-webmail-teams/cdp.js PORT <command>`
  The command set is documented in that skill's SKILL.md. Before launching a new browser, check that skill's `live-session.md` for an authenticated instance from a previous session that may still be running.
- opencode loads these instructions at session start, so already-running sessions keep the old copy until restarted.

## Instruction Feedback

- When a user instruction is clear enough to execute but is materially ambiguous, under-specified, or likely to cause the wrong implementation, provide brief feedback on how the instruction could be written more precisely.
- Give this feedback after completing the requested task unless the ambiguity blocks progress.
- Keep the feedback concise: explain the ambiguity and provide one improved example phrasing.
- Do not give instruction-writing feedback for minor wording issues that do not affect implementation.
